# Machine-generated artifacts whose generator scripts own their own output
# formatting (JSON.stringify / hand-written line writers, not piped through
# Prettier). Running `prettier --write` on these produces a one-time diff
# that `pnpm regen` immediately reverts on the next run — see
# scripts/regen-manifest.mjs FAMILIES for the generators that own these
# paths. Keeping them out of Prettier's scope prevents that ping-pong and
# keeps `pnpm regen`'s `git diff --exit-code` (the Integrity job) the
# single source of truth for their formatting.
packages/rialto/registry.json
packages/rialto-catalog/src/generated-schemas.ts
docs/architecture/dependency-graph.md

# Gitignored build output (packages/rialto-plugin/.gitignore), so it never
# reaches a commit — but CI's build job (`pnpm build`, unscoped) regenerates
# it via `generate-reference.ts` immediately before `repo-audit` runs
# `check:prettier`, so it's present and in-scope for `prettier --check .` at
# that point in the pipeline. Its hand-written markdown table writer doesn't
# match Prettier's table formatting, so leaving it in scope fails CI on a
# clean main with zero code change.
packages/rialto-plugin/generated/component-reference.md

# pnpm-managed lockfile. Prettier's YAML printer doesn't just flip pnpm's
# single-quoted keys to double-quoted (this repo's singleQuote: false) - it
# also reflows some sequences, producing a ~13k-line structural diff on a
# file no one reviews by eye and that pnpm regenerates independently of
# Prettier's config. See .claude/rules/gotchas.md "pnpm-lock.yaml quote
# style diffs are formatting noise".
pnpm-lock.yaml
